WEBVTT

00:00:00.168 --> 00:00:04.705
Once two cameras and a match are known,
the 3D point is where the two rays meet.

00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:09.118
Multiplying out the division in the camera
equation gives two linear equations per
view.

00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:11.380
Four rows, in four unknowns.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:16.605
Take the singular vector with the smallest
singular value and divide by the last
entry.

00:00:16.605 --> 00:00:20.243
In this example the rows vanish at one,
two, four.

00:00:20.243 --> 00:00:22.105
Accuracy depends on the angle:

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:26.268
nearly parallel rays pin down the
direction, but hardly the distance.

00:00:26.232 --> 00:00:30.806
Rectification warps both images as if the
cameras were stepped sideways,

00:00:30.807 --> 00:00:31.633
with no turn.

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:34.170
Now every match sits on the same row.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.820
A point at x on the left appears at x
minus d on the right.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.145
That shift d is the disparity.

00:00:40.145 --> 00:00:42.695
Similar triangles give the key result:

00:00:42.695 --> 00:00:46.557
depth is focal length times baseline, over
disparity.

00:00:46.497 --> 00:00:51.035
A rig has f B of eighty, so disparity
twenty means four metres.

00:00:51.035 --> 00:00:54.760
A half-pixel matching error then costs
about ten centimetres.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.060
What does the same half pixel cost at
twenty metres, five times farther?

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.722
Pause and predict.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:04.044
Two point five metres.

00:01:04.044 --> 00:01:07.152
Five times farther means twenty-five times
the error,

00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:10.320
because depth error grows with the square
of distance.

00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:15.719
One pixel of disparity is twenty
centimetres at four metres, and metres at
twenty.

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:18.747
Doubling the baseline or the focal length
halves it,

00:01:18.748 --> 00:01:20.845
and so does matching more precisely.

00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:22.381
To find d at every pixel,

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:27.263
slide a small window along the same row
and add up the absolute differences.

00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:29.500
Try each disparity in turn.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.287
The cost dips where the windows truly
match.

00:01:32.287 --> 00:01:34.700
Here the minimum is at disparity two.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:39.049
Stacked for every pixel and every d, these
costs form a cost volume,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.376
the structure modern stereo networks still
learn on.
